{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"agent_message","text":"I found a corruption path in the rewrite: a chunked range response can fill the requested span, then send excess bytes that the downloader never reads. I also found inherited issues with conflicting single-stream lengths and unknown-length progress. I’m checking the remaining findings against `before/` and the explicit non-goals."}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"agent_message","text":"There’s also an inherited crash/restart hazard: the single-stream path truncates `.download` without invalidating old segmented state. If a different, larger file is interrupted when its partial length matches the old file’s size, that stale state can later be accepted and produce mixed bytes.\n\nI found no >4 GiB truncation in the rewrite’s offsets or slice conversions. I also checked that an open Rust file handle does not, by itself, make the rename invalid on Windows."}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"agent_message","text":"Read-only review; no files changed or code executed.\n\n1. **High — regression — D1, D5.** `after/segmented_download.rs:237–238`: **An oversized chunked response can produce a wrong completed file.** With no `Content-Length`, suppose a requested N-byte range returns an extra leading byte followed by the requested bytes. The server sends the first N bytes, then the remaining byte separately. `accept()` completes the span and `stream()` returns before reading the excess. Expected: reject or retry the oversized response. Actual: the shifted N-byte prefix can be published successfully. `before/` continued reading and detected the excess.\n\n2. **High — inherited — D1, D4, D5.** `after/segmented_download.rs:434`: **Single-stream truncation leaves segmented state referring to the old contents.** Pause segmented file A, size 8 MiB, with some ranges completed. Start a different 16 MiB file B at the same destination through the single-stream path; kill the app after exactly 8 MiB has been written. Resume A: `load_state()` sees A’s valid checksum and an 8 MiB part file, accepts both, and skips A’s previously completed ranges—which now contain B’s bytes. Expected: invalidate old state before truncating its associated data. Actual: a mixed file can complete. `before/` also left its old metadata/state intact during single-stream writes. This involves differently sized files, not the excluded same-size identity problem.\n\n3. **High — inherited — D1, D5.** `after/segmented_download.rs:430`: **A conflicting single-response length overrides the known full size.** The probe returns `200` with length 80 MB; the subsequent plain GET incorrectly returns the first 40 MB with `Content-Length: 40000000`. Expected: reject the contradiction with the known 80 MB size. Actual: `.or(expected)` selects 40 MB, the final length check passes, and a truncated file is published. `before/` used the same precedence and also lacked the final size check.\n\n4. **Medium — regression — D7.** `after/segmented_download.rs:193–196`, also `:127`, `:368–369`, `:441`: **Blocking filesystem operations can prevent cancellation timers from running.** For example, on a four-thread Tokio runtime, one worker blocks for ten seconds writing to an external drive while three others block acquiring `sh`. The runtime cannot poll the remaining workers’ cancellation timeouts or complete shutdown. Expected: prompt cancellation while other transfers are stalled. Actual: cancellation waits for the blocking I/O. `before/` used asynchronous filesystem operations; it did not impose this runtime-wide blocking through a synchronous mutex.\n\n5. **Medium — regression in the probe retry path — D7.** `after/segmented_download.rs:324–327`, interacting with `:169–171`: **Probe retries do not check cancellation outside timeout expiration.** If cancellation arrives during the first backoff and subsequent connection attempts fail immediately, `wait()` takes its `Ok(Err(...))` branch without inspecting the flag. All remaining backoffs can run, delaying `Ok(None)` by nearly three seconds. Cancellation at the beginning of the final 1.5-second backoff followed by a silent request takes about 2.5 seconds. Expected: about one second. `before/` had no probe retry loop, so this particular delay is new; its uncancellable stalled probe was a separate inherited problem that the rewrite improves. The uninterruptible 1.5-second worker backoff at `:253` is inherited.\n\n6. **Low — regression — D2.** `after/segmented_download.rs:427–428`: **The single path rejects usable successful responses other than 200.** For example, a server returns `203` containing the complete file for both the probe and plain GET. The probe selects `Single`, but `single()` rejects the successful full response. Expected: download a usable full representation. Actual: `Err`. `before/` accepted successful statuses. Rejecting an unsolicited partial `206` is appropriate; the broader rejection also excludes usable responses.\n\n7. **Medium — inherited — D9.** `after/segmented_download.rs:77`, `:475`: **The explicit cookie does not survive a cross-host redirect.** Reqwest strips sensitive headers, including `Cookie`, when following a redirect to another host. If the final URL redirects between hosts that both require the supplied cookie, the redirected request lacks it. Expected under D9’s “every request” requirement: include the cookie. Actual: authentication can fail at the target. `before/` used the same redirect/header arrangement.\n\n8. **Low — inherited — D8.** `after/segmented_download.rs:430–431`, `:454`: **An unknown-length download never reports a completed total.** Let the probe have unknown total and the plain response use chunked encoding without a length. At EOF, N bytes have been received, but the final event remains `{ received: N, total: None }`. Expected: the terminal event reports `Some(N)` and equality. Actual: no event reaches a defined total. `before/` had the same immutable unknown total.\n\n9. **Low — inherited — D1.** `after/segmented_download.rs:490`: **State cleanup failure is silently reported as success.** For example, an existing resumable state file permits reading/writing but Windows denies its deletion, while the part file can be renamed. Expected: success leaves only the destination, or cleanup failure is reported. Actual: `Ok(Some(...))` leaves `.download.state`. `before/` likewise ignored sidecar deletion errors.\n\nChecked and found correct:\n\n- **`wait()` preserves the in-flight future across one-second timeouts.** Passing `&mut fut` does not drop or restart the underlying request each second. Cancellation or final stall exhaustion drops it; the failed stream then drops its response.\n- **Retries are bounded:** four attempts, with 0.5/1/1.5-second backoffs. A persistently silent probe or piece phase takes approximately **123 seconds**. This is not a universal whole-download deadline: successful headers/data and separate probe/download phases can extend elapsed time.\n- Probe transport failures are retried. HTTP error responses return `Err`, consistent with D2.\n- Eight workers issue at most eight concurrent worker requests; the probe response is dropped before workers start.\n- Work stealing, accepted-byte accounting, and checkpoints share one mutex. Independently opened worker files have independent cursors; stolen tails are excluded from the donor’s writes.\n- Workers are aborted **and awaited** before the final checkpoint or rename.\n- Resume loading validates the checksum, marker, total, part length, interval bounds, and overlap. Ordinary torn state is rejected. Fresh segmented initialization removes old state before truncating data.\n- Tens-of-GB offsets remain `u64`; slice conversions are bounded by chunk length. No 4 GiB/`usize` truncation was found within the stated scale.\n- Segmented progress callbacks are serialized and monotone. Known-length successful paths finish at the total.\n- Public signatures, existing derives, and lowercase mode serialization are preserved.\n- Explicit request headers, Chrome user agent, redirect limit, connect timeout, and Korean error prefixes are present, subject to the cookie redirect finding.\n- An open standard Rust file handle is **not inherently a Windows rename bug**: its default sharing permits deletion/rename.\n- No benchmark result can be established by inspection; the referenced `run.sh` was not supplied."}}
